        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "[V2G20-1534] The value of the TimeStamp element of the common MessageHeaderType shall always be in SECC time and encoded at microseconds resolution.",
            "block_bbox": [
                71,
                167,
                1052,
                222
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    71.0,
                    167.0
                ],
                [
                    1052.0,
                    167.0
                ],
                [
                    1052.0,
                    222.0
                ],
                [
                    71.0,
                    222.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 1 The use of microseconds in a 64 bit unsigned long integer has the benefit of preventing the year 2038 problem, allowing for precise synchronization with other high resolution clocks (e.g. as used in smart meters) and allowing precise calculations of clock drift and data transmission or processing latencies in cases where this might be helpful to trace down technical problems.",
            "block_bbox": [
                68,
                242,
                1053,
                340
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    68.0,
                    242.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    242.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    340.0
                ],
                [
                    68.0,
                    340.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 2 While even the EVCC is required to use TimeStamps in SECC time this does not require that it changes its internal hardware clock. The EVCC will only need to calculate its clock offset to SECC time and always apply this delta appropriately. Due to the nature of clock drift that delta can change over time and the EVCC monitors its delta and adjust it continuously.",
            "block_bbox": [
                69,
                360,
                1053,
                458
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    69.0,
                    360.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    360.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    458.0
                ],
                [
                    69.0,
                    458.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "[V2G20-1535]",
            "block_bbox": [
                71,
                479,
                219,
                506
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    71.0,
                    479.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    479.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    506.0
                ],
                [
                    71.0,
                    506.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The value of the TimeStamp element of the common MessageHeaderType sent by the EVCC during the initial SessionSetupReq message shall be its best effort guess of the present SECC time stamp, assuming the SECC time is synchronized to UTC. If the EVCC has no knowledge of the present UTC time than it shall indicate this by sending \"zero\" as the initial TimeStamp.",
            "block_bbox": [
                248,
                479,
                1053,
                608
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    248.0,
                    479.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    479.0
                ],
                [
                    1053.0,
                    608.0
                ],
                [
                    248.0,
                    608.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 3 In cases where the SECC does not have any means to synchronize its clock to external sources (e.g. due to lack of internet connectivity, etc.), the above requirement allows the SECC to decide if it wants to join the EVCC's world view of time.",
            "block_bbox": [
                68,
                631,
                1054,
                704
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    68.0,
                    631.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    631.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    704.0
                ],
                [
                    68.0,
                    704.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "[V2G20-1536]",
            "block_bbox": [
                71,
                726,
                219,
                753
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    71.0,
                    726.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    726.0
                ],
                [
                    219.0,
                    753.0
